### What is this variant of 3Sum?

This is the same 3Sum problem, find all unique triplets summing to zero, approached with the sort-then-two-pointer strategy. The emphasis here is on the pair-search inner loop: after fixing one element, the remaining problem is a sorted two-sum, which the opposite-end two-pointer solves in O(n) per fixed element.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.*;

public class LedgerNettingService {
    public List<List<Integer>> findCancellingTrios(int[] adjustments) {
        Arrays.sort(adjustments);
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        // implementation below
        return result;
    }
}
```

### How does the outer loop work?

The outer loop iterates `i` from 0 to `n - 3`, fixing `adjustments[i]` as the first member of the triplet. If the fixed element equals the previous one, skip it to avoid duplicate triplets. If the fixed element is greater than zero (target is zero), break early because all remaining sums will be positive.

```java
for (int i = 0; i < n - 2; i++) {
    if (i > 0 && adjustments[i] == adjustments[i - 1]) continue;
    if (adjustments[i] > 0) break;
    // inner two-pointer scan
}
```

### How does the inner two-pointer scan work?

For each fixed `i`, set `left = i + 1` and `right = n - 1`. Compute `sum = adjustments[i] + adjustments[left] + adjustments[right]`. If the sum equals zero, record the triplet and skip duplicates on both pointers. If the sum is negative, move left up. If positive, move right down.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.*;

public class ThreeSumPairSearch {
    public static List<List<Integer>> threeSum(int[] a) {
        Arrays.sort(a);
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        int n = a.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 2; i++) {
            if (i > 0 && a[i] == a[i - 1]) continue;
            if (a[i] > 0) break;
            int left = i + 1, right = n - 1;
            while (left < right) {
                int sum = a[i] + a[left] + a[right];
                if (sum == 0) {
                    result.add(List.of(a[i], a[left], a[right]));
                    while (left < right && a[left] == a[left + 1]) left++;
                    while (left < right && a[right] == a[right - 1]) right--;
                    left++;
                    right--;
                } else if (sum < 0) {
                    left++;
                } else {
                    right--;
                }
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] adjustments = {-4000, -2000, -1000, 0, 1000, 2000, 3000, 4000};
        List<List<Integer>> trios = threeSum(adjustments);
        for (List<Integer> trio : trios) {
            System.out.println(trio);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
[-4000, 1000, 3000]
[-4000, 0, 4000]
[-3000, -1000, 4000]
[-3000, 0, 3000]
[-3000, 1000, 2000]
[-2000, -1000, 3000]
[-2000, 0, 2000]
[-1000, 0, 1000]
```

### Why are three duplicate-skip points needed?

Without all three skips, the output contains repeated triplets. The first skip (fixed element) prevents regenerating triplets with the same first value. The second and third skips (left and right after a match) prevent recording the same completing pair within a single fixed-element scan.

```java
// Skip 1: outer loop
if (i > 0 && a[i] == a[i - 1]) continue;

// Skip 2: left pointer after match
while (left < right && a[left] == a[left + 1]) left++;

// Skip 3: right pointer after match
while (left < right && a[right] == a[right - 1]) right--;
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Sorting is O(n log n). The outer loop runs n times, and each inner two-pointer scan is O(n), so the total is O(n squared) which dominates the sort. Extra space is O(1) beyond the sort and the output list. This is a big improvement over the naive O(n cubed) triple loop.

```text
Sort:          O(n log n)
Outer loop:    n iterations
Inner scan:    O(n) each
Total:         O(n * n) = O(n squared)
Extra space:   O(1)
```

### How does this generalise to kSum?

The sort-plus-two-pointer pattern generalises to kSum by recursion. For k greater than 2, fix one element and recurse with k minus 1 on the remaining array. The base case is 2Sum solved with two pointers. The time is O(n to the power of k minus 1).

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.*;

public class KSumSolver {
    public List<List<Integer>> kSum(int[] nums, int target, int k) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        return kSumHelper(nums, target, k, 0);
    }

    private List<List<Integer>> kSumHelper(int[] nums, long target, int k, int start) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (k == 2) {
            int left = start, right = nums.length - 1;
            while (left < right) {
                long sum = nums[left] + nums[right];
                if (sum == target) {
                    result.add(new ArrayList<>(List.of(nums[left], nums[right])));
                    while (left < right && nums[left] == nums[left + 1]) left++;
                    while (left < right && nums[right] == nums[right - 1]) right--;
                    left++; right--;
                } else if (sum < target) left++;
                else right--;
            }
        } else {
            for (int i = start; i < nums.length - k + 1; i++) {
                if (i > start && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue;
                for (List<Integer> sub : kSumHelper(nums, target - nums[i], k - 1, i + 1)) {
                    sub.add(0, nums[i]);
                    result.add(sub);
                }
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is missing the outer-loop duplicate skip, which produces the same triplet from different starting positions. Another is not advancing both pointers after recording a match, which causes an infinite loop or re-records the same triplet.

```java
// Wrong: only advance left, forget right
if (sum == 0) {
    result.add(List.of(a[i], a[left], a[right]));
    left++;
    // BUG: right stays, may re-record
}

// Correct: advance both after skipping duplicates
if (sum == 0) {
    result.add(List.of(a[i], a[left], a[right]));
    while (left < right && a[left] == a[left + 1]) left++;
    while (left < right && a[right] == a[right - 1]) right--;
    left++;
    right--;
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer explains why sorting enables duplicate skipping, proves the three-skip sufficiency, mentions the kSum generalisation, and sorts a copy to preserve the caller's data. They also note that a hash-based 3Sum approach exists but managing duplicates with hashing is harder, making the sort-plus-two-pointer approach the cleaner standard.
